course: dictionary
lesson: 9676
title: Many meanings of one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the word suck has several. To find the right one, read the words around it. «Newborn babies can suck, cry and grip» tells you that suck is something a baby does with its mouth to drink milk, so it means to pull liquid in through the lips.
explain: «Suck a slice of lemon and your whole face screws up.» Here the words slice of lemon show that suck means to keep something in your mouth and move your tongue around it. Try your meaning in the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found the right one.
explain: Watch out for the informal meaning. When someone says a film sucks, they mean it is bad or unpleasant. This is slang, so it is fine to chat with friends, but do not use it in school writing. Also remember that sucked in can mean tricked, as in Australian slang.
fact: Suck means to pull liquid or air into your mouth by tightening your lips and cheeks, as through a straw.
fact: A vacuum cleaner sucks up dust by pulling it in with force.
fact: Mosquitoes suck blood, and bees suck nectar from flowers.
fact: Strong water currents and drains can suck swimmers in, so keep away from pool drains and rip currents.
fact: Informal: «sucks» means bad or unpleasant, so it is slang, not a word for careful writing.
exercise: «Newborn babies can suck, cry and grip.» In this sentence, what does suck mean? | To shout loudly when hungry | *To pull milk into the mouth by tightening the lips and cheeks | To dance slowly | To hold something tightly in the hands
exercise: A vacuum cleaner sucks up dust from the carpet. What does suck up mean here? | *To pull the dust in with force | To keep dust in the mouth and move it around | To drop dust onto the floor | To sweep the dust into a pile
exercise: «Suck a slice of lemon and your whole face screws up.» What does suck mean here? | To pull the lemon into a machine | *To keep the lemon in the mouth and move it around with the tongue | To squeeze the lemon until it is dry | To bite the lemon hard and fast
test: Mosquitoes suck blood, and bees suck nectar. Which meaning of suck fits best? | To chew something until it breaks | *To pull liquid into the mouth, like through a straw | To blow air out in a hurry | To keep something in the mouth and move it around
test: Ella says, «That film sucks.» What does she mean? | The film is very long and slow | *The film is bad or unpleasant | The film is full of bees | The film is about vacuum cleaners
exam: Which sentence uses suck with its informal meaning? | «Babies suck milk from a bottle.» | *«That game totally sucks, it is so boring.» | «Bees suck nectar from the flowers.» | «The vacuum cleaner can suck up dust.»
